Sine Harmonics...\

\b0 This brings up a panel which lets you generate sounds consisting of one or more sine waves.  Each sin wave has a 
\i frequency
\i0  between 0 and 20050 Hertz, an 
\i amplitude
\i0  of between 0 and 1, and a 
\i phase
\i0  between 0 and 2 Pi.  You also specify the 
\i length
\i0  of the generated sound in samples (44100 samples per second of sound).  The 
\b Waves
\b0  pull-down menu lets you put some common sine wave values into the first row.  Press 
\b Generate
\b0  to produce the sound.\
\

\i Suggestion:
\i0  make certain that the amplitudes of all of your sine harmonics add to 
\i less than 1.
\i0   If they add to more than 1 they will often clip and the sound will be distorted.\
\

\pard\tx520\tx1060\tx1600\tx2120\tx2660\tx3200\tx3720\tx4260\tx4800\tx5320\b\fc1\cf1 Other Waves...\

\b0 This brings up a panel which lets you generate sounds consisting of a variety of different non-sine waves, namely square, triangle, sawtooth, pulse, and random waves.  Choose the wave type from the 
\b Wave
\b0  pop-up menu.  Each wave needs a 
\i frequency
\i0  between 0 and 20050 Hertz and an 
\i amplitude
\i0  of between 0 and 1.  You also specify the 
\i length
\i0  of the generated sound in samples (44100 samples per second of sound).   Press 
\b Generate
\b0  to produce the sound.\

